1
00:00:01,002 --> 00:00:02,464
The shop is closed.

2
00:00:02,464 --> 00:00:04,291
The spreadsheets are still open.

3
00:00:04,291 --> 00:00:09,772
QuietOps makes that search a short review
queue, with evidence and a draft ready for

4
00:00:09,772 --> 00:00:10,503
your decision.

5
00:00:10,609 --> 00:00:13,412
Start with the original fictional shop.

6
00:00:13,412 --> 00:00:18,083
Deterministic checks find stock-cover risks,
late deliveries, and invoice candidates.

7
00:00:18,083 --> 00:00:22,288
These findings remain separate from the new
predictive review.

8
00:00:22,399 --> 00:00:23,932
Predictions need history.

9
00:00:23,932 --> 00:00:27,509
The original files contain one stock snapshot.

10
00:00:27,509 --> 00:00:33,130
This optional example adds explicitly
synthetic dated snapshots and earlier sales,

11
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:34,663
preserving the originals.

12
00:00:34,778 --> 00:00:37,175
Run real TabPFN inference locally.

13
00:00:37,175 --> 00:00:43,407
It estimates whether sales during supplier
lead time could exceed stock, assuming no

14
00:00:43,407 --> 00:00:43,886
receipt.

15
00:00:43,886 --> 00:00:47,242
Failed inference never gets a substitute
score.

16
00:00:47,351 --> 00:00:49,655
Rolled oats shows the disagreement.

17
00:00:49,655 --> 00:00:55,184
Both simple baselines show cover, but the
model estimates about eighty-four percent

18
00:00:55,184 --> 00:00:55,645
risk.

19
00:00:55,645 --> 00:01:00,713
This recurring-demand illustration was
selected after exploring model outputs; it is

20
00:01:00,713 --> 00:01:02,556
not an independent test.

21
00:01:02,659 --> 00:01:07,858
The separate historical holdout uses earlier
training and later test dates.

22
00:01:07,858 --> 00:01:13,530
On two hundred and forty synthetic examples,
model F1 reached ninety-seven percent,

23
00:01:13,530 --> 00:01:16,838
against sixty-five and seventy-two for the
baselines.

24
00:01:16,942 --> 00:01:20,773
Inspect the stock snapshot and both sales
files.

25
00:01:20,773 --> 00:01:22,210
Originals stay downloadable.

26
00:01:22,210 --> 00:01:27,478
Estimates are uncalibrated, and sales can hide
demand lost during stockouts.

27
00:01:27,478 --> 00:01:29,873
Real shop performance remains unproven.

28
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:34,189
Now ask local Ministral to explain the
supplied prediction.

29
00:01:34,189 --> 00:01:36,060
TabPFN provides the probability.

30
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:42,141
Ministral explains the facts and suggests a
review step, while guards check source

31
00:01:42,141 --> 00:01:44,479
references, numbers, and uncertain wording.

32
00:01:44,585 --> 00:01:47,832
Create a supplier-review draft and edit the
wording.

33
00:01:47,832 --> 00:01:52,298
Confirm the delivery plan and current stock
before considering a purchase.

34
00:01:52,298 --> 00:01:55,139
The draft still needs a human decision.

35
00:01:55,249 --> 00:01:56,889
Approve for manual export.

36
00:01:56,889 --> 00:02:00,169
QuietOps sends no message and places no order.

37
00:02:00,169 --> 00:02:05,908
Changed data or a new scan date makes
predictive drafts stale, blocking approval and

38
00:02:05,908 --> 00:02:08,778
export until the evidence is reviewed again.

39
00:02:08,892 --> 00:02:13,256
Open models keep inference and shop files on
your machine after setup.

40
00:02:13,256 --> 00:02:17,983
The measured results come from synthetic data,
with no claim of saved minutes.

41
00:02:17,983 --> 00:02:21,983
Less searching, a clearer next step, and you
stay in charge.
